Z||000000||Black88GT||02-13-2001||05:26 PM||||Well I am upgrading the fuel system on my 3.4 GT.  So far here is what I have purchased...<P>- Brand new set of Mustang 19# injectors<BR>- Corvette LT4 fuel pump from GM<BR>- New clips from GM<BR>- Fuel rail o-rings<BR>- Holley Adj. Fuel pressure regulator<BR>- New strainer<P>I am trying to prevent anything that could go wrong with my fuel system.  Any recommendations on what else I should replace while I am at it?  With the fuel tank dropped I would like to get as much done as possible.  <P><BR>TIA<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000001||Tom Philips||02-14-2001||07:50 AM||||Jeff,<BR>What are you trying to accomplish over the stock setup? You will not get any better performance using the production calibration||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||02-14-2001||11:29 AM||||Tom, right now the stock 2.8 injectors are in there and the car has NO fuel over 4000 RPM's.  I believe someone on the fiero.org did a test and came to the conclusion that the stock 2.8 injectors do not provide enough fuel for a 3.4.  I have the Holley regulator so I am not putting too much fuel in there, but I have the oppurtunity to if need be.    <P>Also, in the future a NOS kit or a Turbo kit will be installed so I would like to get the fuel upgrades out of the way.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||12
Z||000003||DRH||02-14-2001||01:39 PM||||New fuel guage sending unit while the tank is out?||||reg||3
Z||000004||Tom Philips||02-14-2001||01:43 PM||||Jeff,<BR>I don't know if you are aware that there is a fuel cutoff built into the calibration i am not sure as to what rpm this happens... also when you go to wot the a/f ratio changes to 12.6:1 this happens when the proper inputs from MAP, TPS, and coolant temp. If you go to Ford injectors make sure you match the pressure spec of those injectors ...   The fuel pressure will rise without any vac on the regulator So there is a normal running spec and a wot spec. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||02-14-2001||03:17 PM||||I was thinking about a fuel gauge sending unit but I heard they were like 100 bucks or something.  Thats too much for just correcting my fuel gauge.<P>Tom, the way I understand it, the fuel cutoff was made to improve gas mileage, and the chip I am buying will eliminate that. <BR>I bought the adj.regulator so I could adjust it for the right settings.  The stock 3.4 injectors are 19# also, but I found brand new mustang ones for an insanely cheap price so I couldn't pass on them.   <BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
